 1 big thing: AI&#x27;s great divergence 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic will make its Wall Street debut today at a moment of profound contradiction in the history of AI, Axios' Zachary Basu writes. 

 The humans are embattled — bogged down by scandal, financial turmoil and political revolt. But the machines are ascendant — accelerating toward an "intelligence explosion" they expect will transform civilization. Why it matters: The industry's recent chaos has obscured a breathtaking surge in technological progress, forcing some of the world's leading researchers to reconsider just how soon AI could surpass human intelligence.

 🔬 Zoom in: Inside Anthropic, soon to be valued north of $1 trillion, independent researchers at METR found that AI models are advancing at twice their historical pace — largely fueled, they believe, by AI itself.

 Anthropic's own data shows Claude went from leading fewer than 1% of the company's AI research tasks in February to more than a quarter by August, with humans still supervising. Zoom out: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei talks about " the exponential ": "an exponential increase in general cognitive capabilities with increasing computing power."

 OpenAI disclosed Tuesday that Astra 6.2 is its first publicly released model to reach the "High" threshold for AI self-improvement — equivalent, the company says, to giving every researcher another highly capable engineer. Data: S&amp;P Capital IQ. Chart: Erin Davis/Axios 🖼️ The big picture: These breakthroughs are pushing some of AI's top forecasters to shorten their timelines for the fabled "intelligence explosion" — a runaway cascade of self-improving AI that could propel machine intelligence far beyond human comprehension.

 The influential AI Futures Project said this month that it now expects machines to surpass the world's best human AI researchers next year. Paul Christiano, an AI safety pioneer who recently joined OpenAI's board, puts the odds of superintelligence emerging within a year at one in three if development continues at its current pace. Between the lines: For all their extraordinary capabilities, the machines remain at the mercy of a fallible human race.

 Take Nvidia: Jensen Huang's sex scandal has plunged the chipmaker into crisis at the very height of its technological and financial power, endangering virtually the entire AI ecosystem. AI leaders' disastrous messaging , veering between promises of utopia and warnings of mass unemployment, has poisoned public trust and helped fuel a revolt against data centers. Share this story . 

 
 
