 2. ⏱️ Axios interview: Ben Sasse on our ticking clock 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Cover: Center Street

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Former U.S. Sen. Ben Sasse, who was told 11 months ago that he had 90 days to live, plans to keep beating the dealer: He'll be out March 9 with what he calls his final book, " Redeem the Time : The Strange Gift of Dying and the Lost Art of Living," co-written with his middle daughter, Alex, who turns 23 today.

 "These are days I wasn't supposed to get," Sasse, 54, told me in a phone interview. 💡 The book's big idea: "We're not just the materially richest people at any time and place in history, but we're also the time-richest people," Sasse says. "And yet, we seem pretty dang miserable. Which is weird, because we ought to be able to make conscious choices about how to invest or redeem the time."

 Sasse, a former centrist Republican senator from Nebraska, told me that after being diagnosed with terminal stage 4 pancreatic cancer, "I just feel unbelievable gratitude that I've gotten this many extra months ... I do everything: I do surgery. I do a lot of high-dose chemo. And I have some high-precision, high-dose radiation starting in 10 days."

 "I feel pretty crummy, probably two-thirds of the time when I'm in treatment," he told me from Gainesville, Fla., where he formerly served as president of the University of Florida. He's being treated at MD Anderson Cancer Center in Houston. "I think we've gotten really good at the up and down of being on morphine: how to manage when you have energy and when you do pain relief," he added. "So today, when I have to be in public, I took a ton of morphine yesterday, slept all day. And today, I'm energetic because I'm on almost no morphine, but I'm in a lot of pain." 📚 I asked Sasse why, with such a loudly ticking shot clock, he decided to invest waning hours in banging out a book. "I was a little reluctant to do it because a cancer memoir sounds so dang boring," he recalled. "So I said no to that. I mean, I'm dying. But everybody's on the clock — there's nothing really that interesting and special in that."

 Sasse said Alex, who's heading to med school next fall, "pushed me to do this as a joint project with her. So that was half of it. And the other half is I'm not dead yet. So why not try to do something to be of use and love to your neighbor and try to serve somebody?" "I'm trying to work halftime-ish, and I get lots of time in hospital hallways where you're just waiting around," he said. "I would rather redeem the time than just sit there and play Tetris on my phone. So we decided to try to redeem the time together." Go deeper . 

 
 
