 8. 🥫 1 for the road: Doomsday boom 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The internet has caught the prepper bug, fueled by fears of AI-led cyberattacks, power-grid vulnerabilities, transportation standstills and international threats, Axios' Avery Lotz writes. 

 At Costco, 21 of 26 big-ticket generators were sold out as of Sunday night, a securities analyst found . Why it matters: More people are embracing the "prepper" mindset — packing emergency bags, stocking up on water and taking inventory of nonperishable food.

 Sales at Survival Frog, a survival retailer that sells gear like emergency food and radios, jumped 450% between Oct. 27 and Nov. 16, compared with prior weeks, CEO and founder Byron Walker tells Axios.

 Competitors saw similar trends, he says. Most recent buyers at Survival Frog were new customers. Food, including canned meats, leads demand, Walker says, followed by products like radios, solar generators, battery packs and water filters. Share this story . 

 
 
